p10 is unmoved, and should be: every mechanism these four chunks touched is a
p2/p6 phenomenon (the tx-type search collapses to one candidate at p10, so
there is no per-trial allocation or per-trial residual to remove there).

And the MEMORY axis over the four, x86 2048 inter, INTERLEAVED `mem_bisect.sh`
(both binaries round-robin in one run, 15 rounds): median 109,332 -> **104,496**
KiB, min 108,820 -> 103,680, max 109,920 -> 104,864 — the distributions do not
overlap, so this is a real **-4.4 %** on the arm the memory goal is stated
against. Peak HEAP is unchanged throughout (61.07 M still / 101.78 M inter).

The same harness at two more x86 cells, and it does NOT all go one way:
    1280 inter  median 45,792 -> **47,000** KiB (min 45,404 -> 46,492, max
                46,132 -> 47,276) — the distributions do not overlap either, so
                this is a real **+2.6 %** (+1.2 MB) at the smaller size.
    2048 still  median 68,856 -> 68,704 (min 68,576 -> 68,236, max 69,140 ->
                68,992) — overlapping; a null.
So the four chunks are -4.4 % on x86 inter at 2048 and +2.6 % at 1280, with the
still arm flat and the heap unmoved at every cell measured. Both directions are
region-retention effects of a changed allocation-size mix, not live bytes; the
aarch64 inter 2048 cell — the one the memory goal is stated against — was
measured per chunk and is flat to +0.01 % (`txout_cshape`), down 1.4 %
(`residual_hoist`) and unmoved by the two after it.

